<html lang="en">
  <head>

    <meta charset="utf-8" />
    <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1" />

    <title>{{seo.title}}</title>

    <!-- Meta tags for SEO and social sharing -->
    <link rel="canonical" href="{{seo.url}}" />
    <meta name="description" content="{{seo.description}}" />
    <meta property="og:title" content="{{seo.title}}" />
    <meta property="og:type" content="article" />
    <meta property="og:url" content="{{seo.url}}" />
    <meta property="og:description" content="{{seo.description}}" />
    <meta property="og:image" content="{{seo.image}}" />
    <meta name="twitter:card" content="summary" />

    <!-- Import the webpage's stylesheet -->
    <link rel="stylesheet" href="/serviceworker_static_routing/style.css" />
    <script src="/serviceworker_static_routing/script.js" defer></script>
  </head>
  <body>
    <div class="wrapper">
      <div class="content" role="main">
        <!-- This is the start of content for our page -->
        <h1>ServiceWorker static routing API demo</h1>

        <!-- Add text indicating that we've changed color OR had an error -->
        <p>
          ServiceWorker static routing API is the new API that allows developers
          to declaratively register routing info at the install phase in
          ServiceWorker. By registering the routing, the browser can get
          response directly from the network, cache storage. Also speed up
          simple offline-first, online-first routes by avoiding ServiceWorker
          startup time.
          <a
            href="https://github.com/WICG/service-worker-static-routing-api/tree/main"
            target="_blank"
          >The explainer</a>
          for more details. Registered routing info in this demo is logged in
          the DevTools console. Also in chrome://serviceworker-internals. For
          the local testing, you can enable the API from
          chrome://flags/#service-worker-static-router if needed.
        </p>
        <div>
          <p>
            <strong>Example links by use cases:</strong>
          </p>
          <ul>
            <li><a href="/serviceworker_static_routing/pages/network">Single URLPattern conditions, source
                from network</a></li>
            <li><a href="/serviceworker_static_routing/pages/fetch-event">Single URLPattern conditions,
                source from fetch handler</a></li>
            <li><a href="/serviceworker_static_routing/pages/cache">Single URLPattern conditions, source from
                cache</a></li>
            <li><a href="/serviceworker_static_routing/pages/race-network-and-fetch-handler">Single
                URLPattern conditions, source from the race between the network
                request and the fetch handler</a></li>
            <li><a
                href="/serviceworker_static_routing/pages/race-network-and-fetch-handler?result=network"
              >Single URLPattern conditions, source from the network, which won
                the race between the network request and the fetch handler</a></li>
            <li><a
                href="/serviceworker_static_routing/pages/race-network-and-fetch-handler?result=fetch-handler"
              >Single URLPattern conditions, source from the fetch handler which
                won the race between the network request and the fetch handler</a></li>

          </ul>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </body>
</html>